#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""stop-wake — stop one agent's running wake, the sanctioned way an operator ends it.

Installed ROOT-OWNED at /opt/basecradle-router/bin/stop-wake (root:root, 0755) by the
NOC's deploy, like every `deploy/bin/*` helper, and run AS ROOT by whoever is stopping
the wake — @origin over SSH, or the NOC under its own account:

    sudo /opt/basecradle-router/bin/stop-wake --agent <slug> [--delivery <id>] [--grace <s>]

WHY IT EXISTS (basecradle-router#386). Killing a runaway wake is an operator's one tool
against it, and until now that meant a hand-typed `pkill`. Two things were wrong with
that. The router read the kill as a crash and re-woke the agent on the same delivery —
fixed in the router, which now reads a stop signal's exit as a stop (`STOP_SIGNALS` in
`basecradle_router.wake`). And a `pkill` is imprecise in both directions: `pkill -f
<binary>` reaches every process whose argv names it, while the session's own children (a
model's tool subprocesses) outlive the kill as orphans. This finds exactly the wake the
daemon launched, stops everything it is running, and records who stopped it.

WHAT IT SIGNALS, AND WHY NOT A PROCESS GROUP. A wake runs as
`router daemon -> sudo -> wake-runner -> systemd-cat -> runuser -> <binary> -> …`, and
none of those links starts a process group: proven on Ubuntu 24.04, the whole wake shares
the DAEMON'S group, so `kill -- -<pgid>` would stop the router itself. So a wake's
members are found two ways:

  * its TREE — the `sudo` the daemon spawned for this agent (its argv is the wake-runner
    invocation, and its parent is the daemon's account), and every descendant by pid;
  * its MARK — every process of the agent's own account whose environment carries this
    wake's `BASECRADLE_DELIVERY_ID`, which wake-runner exports after the privilege drop
    and every descendant inherits. The tree alone misses a process that was already
    reparented — a tool's `nohup job &`, a `setsid` server, a child whose parent exited —
    and the mark survives reparenting and a new session alike. Only a process that
    scrubbed its own environment escapes both. A wake whose delivery id was too odd to
    carry has no mark, and is stopped by its tree alone.

Both are re-read while the wake drains, so a member born in the window is caught too;
whatever outlives the grace period gets SIGKILL. Signalling `sudo` alone would not do: it
relays to `runuser`, which signals only its own child, so the session's grandchildren
survive — the same orphans `pkill` leaves.

THE CHAIN'S RELAYS NEVER DOUBLE A SIGTERM (basecradle-router#404). Two links in
the chain relay a SIGTERM down to their own child, and a second copy of the signal is not
harmless. `runuser` catches the first, SIGTERMs its child, and sleeps 2 s before SIGKILL —
the binary's grace. A second SIGTERM that lands during that sleep interrupts it, and
`runuser` goes straight to SIGKILL (util-linux 2.39 `su-common.c`: the handler stays
installed across the `sleep(2)`). Signalling every member did exactly that: `runuser` got
this op's TERM and `sudo`'s relayed copy, and whether the copy landed before the sleep or
inside it was a race of a few milliseconds; on 2026-11-03 it landed inside it, and an
`@jt` wake was SIGKILLed 3 ms into its grace. So two members are left to the wake's own
relays and sent nothing while it drains:

  * `sudo`, the wake's root. Its relay is the second copy (on the box, ~1.5 ms after it
    receives the TERM). It needs no signal of its own: sudo sends itself the signal that
    ended its command (sudo(8)), so it dies of the TERM that `runuser` re-raises. A
    `use_pty` monitor — a second `sudo` between the two, absent from the box's wakes — is
    a relay of the same kind and is left alone the same way.
  * `runuser`'s child, the binary. `runuser` SIGTERMs it the moment it catches its own, so
    a copy from here would be a second one inside the binary's own handler.

Both are found by their POSITION on the path from the wake's root `sudo` down to
`runuser` — the root's own links before the privilege drop, where nothing of the agent's
session can stand — never by a name or a uid alone. A uid would not do: the wake's `sudo`
is the router account's process, so /proc gives it the router's uid, not root's.

`runuser` is signalled directly rather than through `sudo`, and parents are signalled
before their descendants, so its TERM is pending before anything below it can exit. That
makes the catch deterministic: `runuser` always re-raises, and the daemon always reads a
stop. Left to `sudo`'s relay instead, a binary signalled directly that exited before the
relay arrived would let `runuser` pass its plain status up (0, or 143), which the daemon
reads as a success or a crash — never a stop. The exempt members are still watched, and
SIGKILLed if they outlive the grace period.

Beneath the binary, every member still gets this op's own TERM, because `runuser` relays
to its child alone. A binary that also forwards its TERM to its own children gives them a
second copy; that relay is the binary's, and so is tolerating it.

WHAT THE ROUTER THEN SEES. `sudo` dies by the signal that ended `runuser`, so the daemon
reads exit -15, logs `event=wake_stopped`, never retries, never writes `wake failed`, and
marks the delivery done. That holds even when the binary traps SIGTERM and exits 0, because
`runuser` re-raises a signal it caught, and even when the binary ignores SIGTERM, because
`runuser` SIGKILLs it after its 2 s and still re-raises. A grace period under 2 s cuts
`runuser`'s own: what is left is SIGKILLed with `sudo`, and the daemon reads -9, still a
stop.

What it cannot see is a signal another hand sends `runuser` during the stop: it is no
process's parent here and reads no exit status. The tell is the one the 2026-11-03 stop
left — `duration=` under 2 s on a wake whose `runuser` was running.

WHO STOPPED IT is `SUDO_USER` — asserted by sudo, not by the caller — or the account
running this when it was not reached through sudo. It rides on both lines this writes per
wake, under the journal identifier `basecradle-router-stop-wake`, beside the wake's own
`delivery=` and `agent=`, so one `delivery=<id>` grep reads the stop request, the
router's `event=wake_stopped`, and the agent's own journal together.

ROOT ONLY, AND NOTHING GRANTED. The router daemon is not given this power: no sudoers
rule names this file, so the account that dispatches every wake cannot also kill one.
Root needs no grant, and both callers already hold it.

A PROBE IS NEVER A TARGET: a `--probe` invocation acks and exits without launching
anything, so there is nothing to stop, and it is not matched.

EXIT CODES

    0   stopped     every member of the wake is gone
    1   survived    some process outlived SIGKILL (uninterruptible sleep); named on stdout
    3   no wake     no running wake matched --agent (and --delivery, when given)
    77  not root    EX_NOPERM: it cannot see or signal the wake as anyone else
    2   usage       (argparse)
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import signal
import sys
import syslog
import time
from collections.abc import Callable, Iterable, Mapping
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

WAKE_RUNNER = "/opt/basecradle-router/bin/wake-runner"
ROUTER_USER = "router"
#: The variable wake-runner exports to the agent — `basecradle_router.wake.DELIVERY_ID_ENV`,
#: spelled again because this program runs standalone, and pinned equal by a test.
DELIVERY_ENV = "BASECRADLE_DELIVERY_ID"
IDENT = "basecradle-router-stop-wake"
DEFAULT_GRACE = 10.0
#: After SIGKILL: long enough for the kernel to tear the tree down, short enough that an
#: operator is never left waiting on a process in uninterruptible sleep.
KILL_WAIT = 3.0
POLL = 0.2

EXIT_STOPPED = 0
EXIT_SURVIVED = 1
EXIT_NO_WAKE = 3
EXIT_NOT_ROOT = 77

# The same inert shapes the router and wake-runner pin, so a value that could never
# have been a real wake's is refused rather than silently matching nothing.
_SLUG = re.compile(r"\A[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}\Z")
_DELIVERY = re.compile(r"\A[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{1,128}\Z")
_MARK = f"{DELIVERY_ENV}=".encode()


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Proc:
    """One process, as /proc describes it. ``start`` pins identity across PID reuse."""

    pid: int
    ppid: int
    uid: int
    start: int
    state: str
    argv: tuple[str, ...]
    #: The process's own ``BASECRADLE_DELIVERY_ID``, if its environment carries one.
    delivery: str | None = None
    #: False when its environment could not be read, so whether it carries a mark is unknown.
    marked_known: bool = True

    @property
    def alive(self) -> bool:
        # A zombie has already exited; it is only waiting for its parent to reap it.
        return self.state not in ("Z", "X")


@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Wake:
    """A running wake: the ``sudo`` the daemon spawned, and who and what it is for."""

    root: Proc
    agent: str
    agent_uid: int
    delivery: str | None


def read_proc(proc_root: str, pid: int) -> Proc | None:
    """``pid`` as a :class:`Proc`, or ``None`` if it vanished or cannot be read."""
    base = f"{proc_root}/{pid}"
    try:
        with open(f"{base}/stat", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            stat = fh.read()
        with open(f"{base}/status", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            status = fh.read()
        with open(f"{base}/cmdline", "rb") as fh:
            cmdline = fh.read()
    except OSError:
        return None
    marked_known = True
    try:
        with open(f"{base}/environ", "rb") as fh:
            environ = fh.read()
    except PermissionError:
        # Reading another account's environment takes CAP_SYS_PTRACE, which a real root
        # holds and a capability-trimmed one (a container, a narrowed unit) may not.
        environ, marked_known = b"", False
    except OSError:
        environ = b""  # gone since the stat read: it carries no mark either way
    # comm sits in parentheses and may itself contain spaces or ')', so the fields are
    # read after the LAST ')': state is field 3, ppid 4, starttime 22 (proc(5)).
    tail = stat[stat.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()
    uid = next(
        (int(line.split()[1]) for line in status.splitlines() if line.startswith("Uid:")), -1
    )
    delivery = next(
        (
            var[len(_MARK) :].decode("utf-8", "replace")
            for var in environ.split(b"\0")
            if var.startswith(_MARK)
        ),
        None,
    )
    try:
        return Proc(
            pid=pid,
            ppid=int(tail[1]),
            uid=uid,
            start=int(tail[19]),
            state=tail[0],
            argv=tuple(a.decode("utf-8", "replace") for a in cmdline.split(b"\0") if a),
            delivery=delivery,
            marked_known=marked_known,
        )
    except (IndexError, ValueError):
        return None


def read_procs(proc_root: str = "/proc") -> dict[int, Proc]:
    """Every readable process. Run as root, so the host's ``hidepid`` hides nothing."""
    procs: dict[int, Proc] = {}
    for name in os.listdir(proc_root):
        if name.isdigit():
            proc = read_proc(proc_root, int(name))
            if proc is not None:
                procs[proc.pid] = proc
    return procs


def parse_wake(argv: tuple[str, ...]) -> tuple[str, str | None] | None:
    """``(agent, delivery)`` if ``argv`` is the daemon's real-wake ``sudo`` call, else ``None``.

    The exact argv :class:`~basecradle_router.wake.HomeServerWaker` assembles:
    ``sudo <WAKE_RUNNER> --user <slug> --cwd <clone> [--delivery <id>] -- <command>``.
    A probe (``--probe``) and anything unrecognised before the ``--`` are not wakes.
    """
    if len(argv) < 2 or os.path.basename(argv[0]) != "sudo" or argv[1] != WAKE_RUNNER:
        return None
    user: str | None = None
    delivery: str | None = None
    rest = list(argv[2:])
    while rest:
        flag = rest.pop(0)
        if flag == "--":
            return (user, delivery) if user else None
        if flag not in ("--user", "--cwd", "--delivery") or not rest:
            return None
        value = rest.pop(0)
        if flag == "--user":
            user = value
        elif flag == "--delivery":
            delivery = value
    return None


def find_wakes(
    procs: Mapping[int, Proc],
    agent: str,
    agent_uid: int,
    delivery: str | None,
    router_uid: int,
) -> list[Wake]:
    """The running wakes of ``agent`` (for ``delivery``, when given) the daemon launched."""
    wakes = []
    for proc in procs.values():
        parsed = parse_wake(proc.argv)
        if parsed is None or not proc.alive:
            continue
        who, carried = parsed
        parent = procs.get(proc.ppid)
        if who != agent or parent is None or parent.uid != router_uid:
            continue
        if delivery is not None and carried != delivery:
            continue
        wakes.append(Wake(root=proc, agent=who, agent_uid=agent_uid, delivery=carried))
    return sorted(wakes, key=lambda w: w.root.pid)


def children_of(procs: Mapping[int, Proc], pids: Iterable[int]) -> dict[int, list[int]]:
    """Each parent pid's children among ``pids``, ascending — the one index of the tree."""
    children: dict[int, list[int]] = {}
    for pid in sorted(pids):
        children.setdefault(procs[pid].ppid, []).append(pid)
    return children


def tree(procs: Mapping[int, Proc], roots: Iterable[int]) -> set[int]:
    """``roots`` and every live descendant of them, by parent pid."""
    children = children_of(procs, procs)
    seen: set[int] = set()
    stack = [pid for pid in roots if pid in procs]
    while stack:
        pid = stack.pop()
        if pid in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(pid)
        stack.extend(children.get(pid, ()))
    return {pid for pid in seen if procs[pid].alive}


def members(procs: Mapping[int, Proc], wake: Wake) -> set[int]:
    """Every live process of ``wake``: its tree, and its marked processes with theirs.

    The mark is honoured only on the agent's own account, so nothing another account
    runs can be pulled in by naming a delivery id in its environment.
    """
    marked = (
        [
            p.pid
            for p in procs.values()
            if p.alive and p.uid == wake.agent_uid and p.delivery == wake.delivery
        ]
        if wake.delivery is not None
        else []
    )
    return tree(procs, [wake.root.pid, *marked])


def left_to_relay(procs: Mapping[int, Proc], wake: Wake, pids: Iterable[int]) -> set[int]:
    """The members a stop sends no SIGTERM, because one of the chain's relays already will.

    The relays sit on the path from the wake's root ``sudo`` down to ``runuser``: that
    ``sudo``, whose relayed copy would be a second TERM to ``runuser`` and cut its 2 s
    grace, and any ``sudo`` beneath it on the path (a ``use_pty`` monitor, should a host's
    sudoers ever add that hop, or the root's own child caught between its fork and its
    exec); and ``runuser``, whose children it SIGTERMs itself the moment it catches its own
    (basecradle-router#404). So the exempt are every ``sudo`` on that path and the children
    of the ``runuser`` that ends it. A link on the path that is neither — the wake-runner
    before it execs ``runuser`` — is signalled as any member is.

    A relay is found by POSITION AND NAME: a ``sudo`` or ``runuser`` on that path, never by
    its name or its uid alone, anywhere else in the wake. The path is the root's own
    descendants before the privilege drop, where nothing of the agent's session can stand,
    so a session process that calls itself ``sudo`` or ``runuser`` — even a setuid-root one
    — is just a member and gets its TERM. Nor is it a uid: the wake's ``sudo`` is the router
    account's process, so /proc gives it the router's uid, not root's.
    """
    pids = set(pids)
    children = children_of(procs, pids)

    def named(pid: int, name: str) -> bool:
        argv = procs[pid].argv
        return bool(argv) and os.path.basename(argv[0]) == name

    exempt: set[int] = set()
    path = [wake.root.pid] if wake.root.pid in pids else []
    while path:
        pid = path.pop()
        if named(pid, "runuser"):
            exempt.update(children.get(pid, ()))  # the drop: the agent's own side begins
            continue
        if named(pid, "sudo"):
            exempt.add(pid)
        path.extend(children.get(pid, ()))
    return exempt


def by_depth(procs: Mapping[int, Proc], pids: Iterable[int]) -> list[int]:
    """``pids`` with every ancestor before its descendants — never by pid, which wraps.

    So ``runuser``'s TERM is pending before anything below it is signalled, and it catches
    that TERM even if its child exits at once.
    """

    def depth(pid: int) -> int:
        seen: set[int] = set()
        while pid in procs and pid not in seen:
            seen.add(pid)
            pid = procs[pid].ppid
        return len(seen)

    return sorted(pids, key=lambda pid: (depth(pid), pid))


def fields(**pairs: object) -> str:
    """A ``key=value`` run, dropping empty values — the router's own line grammar."""
    out = []
    for key, value in pairs.items():
        if value is None or value == "":
            continue
        text = str(value)
        if any(c.isspace() for c in text) or '"' in text:
            text = json.dumps(text)
        out.append(f"{key}={text}")
    return " ".join(out)


@dataclass(slots=True)
class _Tracked:
    start: int
    wake: int  # index into the wakes being stopped, so each wake reports its own result
    #: Whether this op has sent it a signal: a member left to a relay is watched, not sent.
    signalled: bool = False


@dataclass(slots=True)
class Stopper:
    """Stop the matched wakes — every collaborator injectable, so it is testable offline."""

    read: Callable[[], dict[int, Proc]]
    kill: Callable[[int, int], None]
    log: Callable[[str], None]
    sleep: Callable[[float], None] = time.sleep
    now: Callable[[], float] = time.monotonic
    _tracked: dict[int, _Tracked] = field(default_factory=dict)

    def stop(self, wakes: list[Wake], *, by: str, grace: float) -> list[int]:
        """Stop every member of every wake — SIGTERM where no relay delivers it, then
        SIGKILL to whatever outlives ``grace`` — and return the pids that survived SIGKILL."""
        started = self.now()
        procs = self.read()
        for wake in wakes:
            self.log(
                fields(
                    event="wake_stop_requested",
                    delivery=wake.delivery,
                    agent=wake.agent,
                    by=by,
                    signal="TERM",
                    pids=len(members(procs, wake)),
                )
            )
        self._sweep(procs, wakes, signal.SIGTERM)

        while self.now() - started < grace:
            self.sleep(POLL)
            procs = self.read()
            if not self._sweep(procs, wakes, signal.SIGTERM) and not self._live(procs):
                break

        # Escalate: anything still standing, and any member born since the last sweep.
        procs = self.read()
        killed = self._live(procs)
        for pid in killed:
            self._send(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        killed += self._sweep(procs, wakes, signal.SIGKILL)
        survivors = killed
        waited = 0.0
        while survivors and waited < KILL_WAIT:
            self.sleep(POLL)
            waited += POLL
            survivors = self._live(self.read())

        elapsed = f"{self.now() - started:.1f}s"
        for index, wake in enumerate(wakes):
            own = {pid for pid, t in self._tracked.items() if t.wake == index}
            own_signalled = [pid for pid in own if self._tracked[pid].signalled]
            own_killed = sorted(own.intersection(killed))
            own_survivors = sorted(own.intersection(survivors))
            outcome = "survived" if own_survivors else ("killed" if own_killed else "stopped")
            self.log(
                fields(
                    event="wake_stop_done",
                    delivery=wake.delivery,
                    agent=wake.agent,
                    by=by,
                    outcome=outcome,
                    signalled=len(own_signalled),
                    killed=len(own_killed),
                    survivors=" ".join(map(str, own_survivors)) or None,
                    duration=elapsed,
                )
            )
        return survivors

    def _live(self, procs: Mapping[int, Proc]) -> list[int]:
        """The tracked processes still running — the same process, never a reused pid."""
        return sorted(
            pid
            for pid, t in self._tracked.items()
            if pid in procs and procs[pid].start == t.start and procs[pid].alive
        )

    def _sweep(self, procs: Mapping[int, Proc], wakes: list[Wake], sig: int) -> list[int]:
        """Track every member not yet tracked, recording which wake it belongs to, and
        signal it — unless it is a SIGTERM a relay will deliver (:func:`left_to_relay`).
        SIGKILL is never left to a relay: nothing relays it.

        A tracked member left to a relay is looked at again on every sweep, because what a
        pid is can change under it: a ``sudo`` child caught between its fork and its exec
        is a relay at the first sweep and ``runuser`` at the next, and ``runuser``'s child
        whose ``runuser`` has gone has no relay left. Each is signalled once it is no
        longer exempt. Returns the members tracked for the first time."""
        fresh = []
        for index, wake in enumerate(wakes):
            pids = members(procs, wake)
            relayed = left_to_relay(procs, wake, pids) if sig == signal.SIGTERM else set()
            for pid in by_depth(procs, pids):
                tracked = self._tracked.get(pid)
                if tracked is None:
                    tracked = self._tracked[pid] = _Tracked(start=procs[pid].start, wake=index)
                    fresh.append(pid)
                elif tracked.signalled or tracked.start != procs[pid].start:
                    continue  # already sent its one signal, or a reused pid: never ours
                if pid not in relayed:
                    self._send(pid, sig)
        return fresh

    def _send(self, pid: int, sig: int) -> None:
        self._tracked[pid].signalled = True
        # A process that exited between the read and the signal is the outcome we wanted.
        with contextlib.suppress(ProcessLookupError):
            self.kill(pid, sig)


def _operator() -> str:
    """Who is stopping the wake: sudo's own record of the caller, else this account."""
    sudo_user = os.environ.get("SUDO_USER", "")
    if _SLUG.match(sudo_user):
        return sudo_user
    try:
        return pwd.getpwuid(os.getuid()).pw_name
    except KeyError:
        return str(os.getuid())


def _journal(line: str) -> None:
    """The line on stdout for the operator, and in the journal under :data:`IDENT`.

    ``syslog`` reports no failure, so stdout is the record the operator can always see;
    the journal copy is what joins it to the router's and the agent's lines.
    """
    print(line, flush=True)
    syslog.syslog(syslog.LOG_WARNING, line)


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="stop-wake", description="Stop an agent's running wake (an operator's stop)."
    )
    parser.add_argument("--agent", required=True, help="the agent's OS-user slug")
    parser.add_argument("--delivery", help="stop only the wake for this delivery id")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--grace",
        type=float,
        default=DEFAULT_GRACE,
        help=f"seconds to wait after SIGTERM before SIGKILL (default {DEFAULT_GRACE:g})",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if not _SLUG.match(args.agent):
        parser.error(f"--agent is not an agent slug: {args.agent!r}")
    if args.delivery is not None and not _DELIVERY.match(args.delivery):
        parser.error(f"--delivery must match [A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{{1,128}}: {args.delivery!r}")
    if args.grace < 0:
        parser.error("--grace must be >= 0")

    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        print(
            "stop-wake: must run as root (sudo); it cannot see or signal a wake otherwise",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return EXIT_NOT_ROOT
    target = f"agent={args.agent}" + (f" delivery={args.delivery}" if args.delivery else "")
    try:
        router_uid = pwd.getpwnam(ROUTER_USER).pw_uid
        agent_uid = pwd.getpwnam(args.agent).pw_uid
    except KeyError:
        print(f"stop-wake: no running wake matched {target} (no such account)", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_NO_WAKE

    procs = read_procs()
    wakes = find_wakes(procs, args.agent, agent_uid, args.delivery, router_uid)
    if not wakes:
        print(f"stop-wake: no running wake matched {target}", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_NO_WAKE

    unknown = sum(1 for p in procs.values() if p.uid == agent_uid and not p.marked_known)
    if unknown:
        # Loud, never silent: the stop still runs, by the tree, but a member already
        # reparented out of it would be missed, and the operator must know to look.
        print(
            f"stop-wake: WARNING: could not read {unknown} of {args.agent}'s process "
            "environments (no CAP_SYS_PTRACE?): a member already reparented out of the "
            f"wake's tree will be missed — check `ps -u {args.agent}` afterwards",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
    syslog.openlog(IDENT, 0, syslog.LOG_DAEMON)
    stopper = Stopper(read=read_procs, kill=os.kill, log=_journal)
    survivors = stopper.stop(wakes, by=_operator(), grace=args.grace)
    return EXIT_SURVIVED if survivors else EXIT_STOPPED


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
